WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.549 --> 00:00:05.519
WandaVision is full of references and jokes
that will soar over the heads of young comics

00:00:05.519 --> 00:00:07.680
readers and MCU fans.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.110
But the adults watching will know what's what
— especially if they grew up glued to their

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:13.330
TV sets.

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:17.550
Here are a few things only adults likely noticed
in WandaVision.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:23.230
The Dick Van Dyke Show is one of the most
influential sitcoms of all time, but kids

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:29.029
today likely don't know much about it, considering
it debuted way back in 1961.

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:34.329
Comedy writer Rob and his wife Laura, played
by TV legends Dick Van Dyke and Mary Tyler

00:00:34.329 --> 00:00:39.760
Moore, have a chemistry similar to that of
Wanda and Vision's, especially in the Disney+

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:41.489
hit's first two episodes.

00:00:41.489 --> 00:00:46.329
The living room and entryway of WandaVision's
black-and-white suburban house is even laid

00:00:46.329 --> 00:00:50.929
out the same as Rob and Laura's, with the
kitchen on the far right, and the front door

00:00:50.929 --> 00:00:52.200
on the far left.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:57.039
There's one obvious Dick Van Dyke Show allusion
in WandaVision that younger viewers likely

00:00:57.039 --> 00:01:02.749
missed: In the faux-sitcom's opening credits
sequence, Vision carries Wanda over the threshold

00:01:02.749 --> 00:01:05.390
and nearly trips on a poorly placed chair.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:10.440
Instead, he glides right through it, because
as a synthezoid, that's something that he

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:11.440
can do.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:16.040
On The Dick Van Dyke Show, Rob typically fell
over an ottoman as he entered the house each

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.520
week, but sometimes he dodged it.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:23.670
By Episode 2 of WandaVision, we're firmly
in the 1960s, and the connection to The Dick

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.400
Van Dyke Show is even more clear.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.920
Wanda has dropped her demure dress, apron,
and heels in favor of sleek black capri pants

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.810
and more comfortable shoes.

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:38.640
Add her stylish flip hairdo into the mix,
and she looks just like Mary Tyler Moore as

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:39.640
Laura.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.090
Another vintage sitcom that adults are more
likely to be familiar with is Bewitched.

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:51.620
The high-concept sitcom, which ran from 1964
to 1972, concerned a witch named Samantha

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:53.930
who married a befuddled guy named Darrin.

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:55.760
"I am a witch."

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:01.110
"What, um, what makes you say that?"

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:06.130
There was no way that WandaVision, a sci-fi
story told through old TV comedy tropes in

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:11.640
which one of its two main characters is a
witch, wouldn't send up or embrace Bewitched.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:16.610
With a point of a finger, Wanda changes the
"for sale" sign on a chosen house into "sold,"

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:19.840
a very Samantha act of instantaneous magic.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:25.000
Also like Samantha, she uses her amazing powers
to accomplish household tasks, like doing

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:30.310
the dishes and making dinner, the latter of
which she accomplishes by manifesting objects

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:31.640
out of thin air.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:37.170
When his boss comes over for dinner, Vision
virtually transforms into the hapless Darrin.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:41.730
Just as that character was often left desperately
trying to keep hidden the fact that his wife

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:46.870
was a witch, Vision vamps, sings, and tries
other distraction methods to make sure his

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:52.650
boss and his wife don't see pots, pans, and
food floating through the air in the kitchen.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:58.120
In Episode 3 of WandaVision, the action shifts
to the early 1970s.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:03.150
More specifically, their world turns into
a detailed re-creation of one of the most

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:06.440
definitive sitcoms of the era: The Brady Bunch.

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:10.640
From the beginning of the opening sequence,
viewers old enough to remember the adorably

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:16.520
cornball comedy about a huge blended family
are bombarded with a bunch of Brady-like bits

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:17.520
back-to-back.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.650
"Marcia, Marcia, Marcia!"

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:24.680
The title sequence depicts Vision engaging
in mid-century suburban activities like grilling

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:29.630
burgers and putting together a swing set,
and he's doing them both outside, past a sliding

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:36.430
glass door in a tiny, unnaturally well-lit
yard atop fake grass — it's a very Brady

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:37.430
yard.

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:42.140
The inside of the house also invites Brady
comparisons, such as the double front doors,

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:46.120
brick and stone walls, and open, platform-style
stairs.

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:53.140
The plot of WandaVision's third episode revolves
around Wanda's sudden, unexpected, and extremely

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:55.330
rapidly progressing pregnancy.

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:59.960
As that occurs in the environment of a classic
sitcom, the show embraces and sends up the

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:04.360
many tired clichés frequently foisted upon
pregnant characters.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:09.400
Like many sitcom dads-to-be before him, Vision
tries to prepare for the baby's arrival with

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.970
logic and knowledge, devouring books on pregnancy.

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:17.349
He's a good source of info for Wanda, teaching
her about things like breathing exercises

00:04:17.349 --> 00:04:22.199
to deal with pain — just like other comedy
characters who would attend a Lamaze or general

00:04:22.199 --> 00:04:23.800
childbirth class.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:25.690
"Mucus plug!

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:29.469
In your face, pal!"

00:04:29.469 --> 00:04:34.680
In decades past, the big dramatic moment in
soaps and sitcoms that lets the audience know

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:39.930
the birth is imminent is when the mother's
water breaks, and that happens to Wanda, although

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:44.919
WandaVision subverts the idea — it comes
in the form of a torrential indoor rainstorm.

00:04:45.919 --> 00:04:50.900
The fifth episode of WandaVision takes place
in the '80s, and the sitcom of that era it

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:57.190
most explicitly emulates is Family Ties, the
smash hit about the Keatons, former '60s hippies

00:04:57.190 --> 00:05:01.710
who move to the suburbs to raise their conservative
and superficial '80s kids.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:03.569
"This is a travesty!

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:07.270
This is a sin against Capitalism!"

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:11.759
WandaVision's set in this episode is dressed
to resemble the Keatons' Columbus, Ohio, home,

00:05:11.759 --> 00:05:17.830
what with the dark wood flourishes, small
glass windows, and central yellow couch.

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:23.190
Vision even rocks Steven Keaton's usual wardrobe
of a plaid shirt tucked into jeans.

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:27.610
One of the most memorable elements of Family
Ties, for those old enough to remember, was

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:32.199
an opening sequence in which a line drawing
portrait of the Keatons is slowly filled in

00:05:32.199 --> 00:05:33.569
with paint.

00:05:33.569 --> 00:05:38.240
WandaVision's credit sequence re-creates that,
albeit with an independently moving magic

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:40.550
wand-like brush doing the painting.

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:46.569
Family portrait painting aside, the opening
sequence of the '80s-sitcom-style episode

00:05:46.569 --> 00:05:51.789
of WandaVision is all about another hit show
from that period: Growing Pains.

00:05:51.789 --> 00:05:57.169
That show, ABC's answer to NBC's The Cosby
Show, is also about a wealthy Huntington,

00:05:57.169 --> 00:06:00.800
Long Island family in which the father is
a doctor.

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:05.379
Older WandaVision viewers will remember Growing
Pains' opening credit sequence, which the

00:06:05.379 --> 00:06:09.389
MCU show re-creates while lovingly poking
fun at the whole thing.

00:06:09.389 --> 00:06:14.229
Set to a theme song like the one from Growing
Pains, with mawkish and overly sentimental

00:06:14.229 --> 00:06:19.869
lyrics about family and love sung over bright
electric guitar riffs, the visuals consist

00:06:19.869 --> 00:06:26.210
of photos of Wanda, Vision, and family growing
up... just like its '80s sitcom inspiration.

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:30.509
Growing Pains' visual style makes one more
appearance toward the end of the episode.

00:06:30.509 --> 00:06:36.020
Wanda, not wanting to listen to Vision confront
her about her cruel manipulation of time,

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:41.490
space, and lives, makes her TV life's credits
roll, and they're in the same typeface as

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:42.909
those from Growing Pains.

00:06:43.909 --> 00:06:49.659
In Wanda's concocted sitcom world of Westview,
much of the mystery focuses on her twin sons,

00:06:49.659 --> 00:06:50.819
Tommy and Billy.

00:06:50.819 --> 00:06:56.509
The twins exist in the comics, but on WandaVision,
especially during Episode 5, they're a vehicle

00:06:56.509 --> 00:07:00.619
to send up the presentation of children in
'80s TV comedies.

00:07:00.619 --> 00:07:04.830
At the beginning of the fifth installment
of the series, Tommy and Billy are infants,

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:09.759
by the middle they're kindergarten age, and
they're ten years old by the time the episode

00:07:09.759 --> 00:07:10.969
concludes.

00:07:10.969 --> 00:07:15.699
Their rapid growth is both a function of Wanda's
magic and reflective of how sitcoms in the

00:07:15.699 --> 00:07:17.919
'80s routinely did this.

00:07:17.919 --> 00:07:23.360
For example, Andrew Keaton on Family Ties
and Chrissy Seaver on Growing Pains both went

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:28.999
from baby to wisecracking grade-schooler in
just a few months, and their rapid aging was

00:07:28.999 --> 00:07:30.069
never acknowledged.

00:07:31.279 --> 00:07:37.009
Speaking of twins, the most popular '80s sitcom
involving identical children is definitely

00:07:37.009 --> 00:07:38.439
Full House.

00:07:38.439 --> 00:07:43.039
Episode 5 of WandaVision includes a few references
to that family-friendly hit.

00:07:43.039 --> 00:07:47.960
When anybody does anything sentimental or
cute on WandaVision, the studio audience emits

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:53.880
a treacly "aww," for example, and toddler-aged
Tommy and Billy are reminiscent of Nicky and

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:57.689
Alex, Uncle Jesse and Aunt Becky's tiny twins.

00:07:57.689 --> 00:08:02.779
In the opening credit sequence, Wanda, Vision,
and their kids run through the rolling field

00:08:02.779 --> 00:08:07.629
in the town square toward the camera and end
the sequence with a picnic, captured in a

00:08:07.629 --> 00:08:08.729
wide shot.

00:08:08.729 --> 00:08:13.590
Both of those moments are instantly recognizable
to viewers of a certain age as straight out

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:15.169
of the Full House opening.

00:08:15.169 --> 00:08:17.440
This is all amusing on another level, too.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:22.879
Michelle Tanner on Full House was portrayed
by twins Mary Kate and Ashley Olsen, the older

00:08:22.879 --> 00:08:27.069
sisters of Elizabeth Olsen... who stars as
Wanda on WandaVision.

00:08:28.069 --> 00:08:32.919
By Episode 6 of WandaVision, Wanda Maximoff
is still living in a television-based alternate

00:08:32.919 --> 00:08:38.229
world of her own creation, which evolves each
week to correspond to an increasingly recent

00:08:38.229 --> 00:08:39.599
real-life sitcom.

00:08:39.599 --> 00:08:44.560
In this installment, the show moves from the
'80s and early '90s tropes of Family Ties

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:50.000
and Growing Pains to get to the edgier, rougher,
single-camera, laugh track-free comedies of

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:53.190
the 2000s, particularly Malcolm in the Middle.

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:58.350
The beginning of WandaVision's sixth episode
is packed with references to the Fox hit,

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:02.529
which debuted in January of 2000 — a bit
before younger viewers' time.

00:09:02.529 --> 00:09:08.430
The opening sequence of the older show featured
harried mom Lois caught in a towel, and WandaVision's

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:14.380
intro segment — also shot frenetically and
with lots of video feedback and footage discoloration

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:19.740
— finds Wanda brushing her teeth, not thrilled
about getting captured on a camcorder.

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:24.860
Character names, presented as credits, appear
in a similar font as the one utilized on Malcolm.

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:29.550
Then, once the action of the show starts,
Billy and Tommy break the fourth wall and

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:34.860
address the audience directly, a device employed
by Malcolm throughout that series' run.

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:40.430
Jaunty ska-flavored music plays lightly on
the soundtrack as they do, a nod to Malcolm's

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:42.000
similar score.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:46.550
WandaVision also emulates Malcolm's visual
style here, employing the use of quick wipe

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:48.570
cuts and odd camera angles.

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:54.649
Because WandaVision is a sitcom — or at
least a show set in an artificial, sitcom-derived

00:09:54.649 --> 00:09:57.170
world — it gets a Halloween episode.

00:09:57.170 --> 00:10:03.520
The MCU series' sixth installment finds Wanda,
Vision, the twins, and Pietro celebrating

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:08.180
the big fall holiday, and they dress up for
the occasion in form-fitting, brightly-colored

00:10:08.180 --> 00:10:09.779
Halloween costumes.

00:10:09.779 --> 00:10:14.980
Their outfits are too cheap, cheesy, and flashy
to not be designed to catch older viewers'

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:19.779
attention, and to make them search their brains
as to why those outfits seem so familiar.

00:10:19.779 --> 00:10:23.110
The reason is also an in-joke for Marvel Comics
fans.

00:10:23.110 --> 00:10:29.500
Wanda, a.k.a. Scarlet Witch, and Vision, mostly
hiding their superhero identities, don't often

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:32.740
appear on WandaVision in anything like a superhero
costume.

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:38.220
But on Halloween, they dress up in re-creations
of the superhero costumes they wore in Marvel

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:41.329
Comics adventures published long ago.

00:10:41.329 --> 00:10:46.870
In print, Wanda wore a pointy headpiece, cape,
and leotard, just like she does here, while

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:51.610
Vision's green bodysuit and yellow cape is
also comics-appropriate.

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:56.379
Even Pietro gets in on the fun, donning a
blue T-shirt with a silver stripe and hair

00:10:56.379 --> 00:11:02.259
fluffed upward, resembling the inked version
of his super alter ego Quicksilver.

00:11:02.259 --> 00:11:07.230
The term "breaking the fourth wall" refers
to when an actor addresses viewers directly,

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:09.790
dissolving the invisible barrier between worlds.

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:14.870
It can also be used to describe that thing
in 21st century mockumentary sitcoms where

00:11:14.870 --> 00:11:19.540
characters appear in confessional segments
to explain their thoughts and actions, or

00:11:19.540 --> 00:11:23.480
turn to the camera to react to the behavior
of others.

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:27.959
Those are all tropes of Modern Family, and
the seventh episode of WandaVision, "Breaking

00:11:27.959 --> 00:11:32.839
the Fourth Wall," is full of homages to the
Emmy-winning ABC smash.

00:11:32.839 --> 00:11:37.500
Wanda is starting to crack under the pressure
of maintaining a faux sitcom world, and she

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:43.019
decides to grab some "me time," all of which
she discusses by talking right to the camera,

00:11:43.019 --> 00:11:48.160
which fidgets ever so slightly and jaggedly
zooms in on her face every so often to mimic

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:50.519
the handheld style of Modern Family.

00:11:50.519 --> 00:11:55.160
Her "talking head" segment is delivered from
a striped chair that WandaVision producers

00:11:55.160 --> 00:11:59.689
seemingly took right off the set of Modern
Family, which also inspired this episode's

00:11:59.689 --> 00:12:04.130
look, with its chic interior design and expensive-looking
furniture.

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:09.779
The jazzy big-band drums on the soundtrack
also remind older viewers of that popular

00:12:09.779 --> 00:12:15.380
sitcom, as do Vision and Agnes silently expressing
alarm over Wanda's actions for the benefit

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:16.380
of the viewer.

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:20.970
The opening credit sequence borrows Modern
Family's typeface at the end, but the style

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:26.410
— with rapid-fire examples of "Wanda" appearing
on real-life objects — comes straight from

00:12:26.410 --> 00:12:30.899
Happy Endings, a sitcom that once came on
right after Modern Family.

00:12:30.899 --> 00:12:35.899
It also brings to mind the US version of The
Office, another famous fourth-wall-breaker,

00:12:35.899 --> 00:12:40.450
with the "I Heart Wanda" mug and the similar
theme song.

00:12:40.450 --> 00:12:43.120
